```c
#include <iostream>

using namespace std;

void bubbleSort(int arr[], int n) {
    int i, j, temp;

    for (i = 0; i < n-1; i++) {
        for (j = 0; j < n-i-1; j++) {
            if (arr[j] > arr[j+1]) {
                temp = arr[j];
                arr[j] = arr[j+1];
                arr[j+1] = temp;
            }
        }
    }
}

int main() {
    int arr[] = {4, 5, 2, 7, 1, 8, 9};
    int n = sizeof(arr) / sizeof(arr[0]);
    bubbleSort(arr, n);
    cout << "Sorted array: \n";
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        cout << arr[i] << " ";
    }
    cout << endl;
    return 0;
}
```
This code sorts the array `arr` in ascending order using the Bubble Sort algorithm. The `bubbleSort` function takes in an array and its size as arguments and sorts the array using nested for loops. The outer loop iterates from left to right, while the inner loop iterates from right to left. If the current element is greater than the next element, they swap their positions. The algorithm repeats until there are no more elements to sort.

In the `main` function, an example array is defined and passed to the `bubbleSort` function along with its size. The sorted array is then displayed on the screen.

